message.reasoning_content:
We have a codebase for a local-first freelance finance tracker. Acceptance tests passed, but a UI flow test (primary flow) failed due to ExportScreen not showing the share sheet indicator "Export ready to share". The UI flow test expects after clicking Export CSV button, an "Export ready to share" message appears (snackbar). The test monitors locator.waitFor expecting that text appears. The static validation UI primary flow check gave:

" The designer's primary flow (ui_flow.json, read-only) must work at phone and desktop size; labels and button names come from DESIGN.md:
phone: step 51 'expect \"Export ready to share\"' failed: locator.waitFor: Timeout 10000ms exceeded.
  on page /; previous steps ok: ['click button \"Export CSV\"', 'expect \"Export ready to share\"', 'click button \"Export PDF\"']
  visible text: Export data Export data Back Export CSV Export PDF
  failed API calls: none; page errors: []"

It says after clicking Export CSV and Export PDF, the "Export ready to share" text didn't appear. However in the ExportScreen component we have a Snackbar displayed based on state variable snack, set to "Export ready to share" after async generateCsv or generatePdf functions succeed. In the tests (ui_flow?) they simulate clicking the button and check for "Export ready to share". Perhaps the async functions return quickly? In jest environment, they'd succeed. But UI flow test runs the actual app (maybe using Playwright). The generateCsv function does many file IO but test environment may not have necessary storage permission or failing. The generateCsv code tries to import storage module dynamically and get the store. If that fails (maybe context of web?), they'd catch errors? They have try/catch for dynamic import; they catch any error and set s = null; then if s is null, they will catch => fallback to empty arrays, then generate CSV string. That likely works.

However generatePdf uses expo-print, expo-file-system, which may not work in web environment used for UI flow tests. In UI flow test, they click Export CSV, then see "Export ready to share". That expectation fails. But based on component code, after clicking Export CSV button, generateCsv() is called, which may be async but quick. Then setLoading true, then after await completes, setSnack('Export ready to share') and setLoading false. That should cause a Snackbar with that text.

But screenshot shows visible text: Export data Export data Back Export CSV Export PDF - the Snackbar is not shown. Possibly because the ExportButton's onPress returns void but the component defines exportCsv as a function within component; however the ExportButton component imported expects `onPress` prop to be a function that returns Promise<void> or void. ExportButton returns a Button from react-native-paper. That should call onPress. So why the snackbar not showing? Perhaps the SnackBar component is not visible because the exported code includes missing import of PrimaryButton from ../components/ui; but they used PrimaryButton from ../components/ui; PrimaryButton is exported from components/ui/index.ts as PrimaryButton. That likely works. But maybe the ExportScreen component uses PrimaryButton with prop variant="secondary". The PrimaryButton component signature expects label property; but they used `<PrimaryButton label="Back" variant="secondary" onPress={...} />`. Let's examine PrimaryButton implementation: file src/components/ui/PrimaryButton.tsx:

